## Cross-arm grouping correction for #649 F1

The unchanged #649 probe reproduced both coordinated case/pair splits: each passed linkage and counted one structurally complete synthetic comparison, although its arms belonged to different groups. The correction adds an explicit comparison declaration to the local design-only plan; the old record/manifest envelopes and legacy validators remain unchanged.

A group is identified by `case_id` and `pair_id`. The sole `plan.comparisons` entry declares these identities and a `repetitions` map from each opposite arm to its planned `repetition_id`. Exactly two `plan.slots` must share the group, contain one `no-tool` and one `tool` arm, use distinct record IDs and match that repetition map. Every record and unique attempt must match all five slot identity fields. This retains the old fixture's shared repetition label while allowing the legacy runner's arm-specific repetition labels through an explicit map; equality of cross-arm repetition strings is not a general rule. Repetition identities never identify retries or attempts. No real repetition allocation or historical runner audit is performed.

Completion is derived from that validated group and its corresponding unique records and attempts, after legacy validation and proposed linkage pass. Both records and answers must be complete with no errors, failed tool calls, truncation or exhaustion. Missing, duplicate, mismatched, interrupted or failed arms, or invalid plan grouping, yield zero completion; planned/present/retained/interrupted inventories remain visible. Changing a slot's repetition consistently in its record and attempt but outside the declaration rejects. A fully declared alternative repetition map is structurally valid only; matching hashes do not authorize changing any frozen real plan.

All 60 expected decisions and accounting outcomes were saved before the corrected execution: the original 49 linkage expectations remain, with explicit accounting now required for every control. Eleven added controls reuse the saved coordinated case/pair counterexamples and cover an unmapped repetition, valid arm-specific repetition mapping, alternative group identities, duplicate slot/arm/record, missing planned arm/comparison declaration and a retained failed arm. Existing positive, missing, duplicate, unsupported and intact/empty/malformed interrupted-response expectations are preserved. The original counterexample inputs remain unchanged; corrected-format controls add the base comparison declaration and refresh dependent plan bytes/hashes.

